---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro'
import Section from '../components/Section.astro'
import ButtonLink from '../components/ButtonLink.astro'
import { INTEGRATIONS, TDARR } from '../data/integrations'

/*
 * The most carefully worded page on the site.
 *
 * Streamarr reads a library the operator has already organised, using tools the
 * operator already runs. It performs no indexing and obtains no media. Every
 * sentence here must stay on that side of the line — see
 * docs/artifacts/streamarr-legal-release.html.
 */
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Integrations"
  description="Streamarr reads the library your existing management tools have already organised — Sonarr, Radarr, Lidarr, Readarr, Bazarr and Prowlarr — and dispatches background encoding to Tdarr."
>
  <Section
    eyebrow="Integrations"
    title="Works with the tools you already run"
    lede="If you keep your library tidy with the *arr applications, Streamarr reads the result. It does not replace them, it does not duplicate them, and their own interfaces carry on working exactly as before."
  >
    <div class="statement">
      <p>
        <strong>What Streamarr takes from them:</strong> the titles, the file paths those titles
        resolve to, and the metadata identifiers already established. That is what turns a folder
        of files into a catalogue with correct posters, correct episode ordering and correct cast
        lists.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>What Streamarr does not do:</strong> it performs no indexing, runs no searches
        against anything outside your own library, and obtains no media. It is a playback,
        catalogue and metadata layer over storage you already control.
      </p>
    </div>
  </Section>

  <Section tint title="Library managers">
    <div class="scroll-x">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Application</th>
            <th scope="col">Covers</th>
            <th scope="col">What Streamarr reads</th>
            <th scope="col">If it is not connected</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {
            INTEGRATIONS.map((integration) => (
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">{integration.name}</th>
                <td>{integration.role}</td>
                <td>{integration.reads}</td>
                <td class="muted">{integration.without}</td>
              </tr>
            ))
          }
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="after">
      Worth being plain about: these connections are not optional garnish. Streamarr has no
      filesystem scanner of its own — it builds its catalogue entirely from what these
      applications have already reconciled. A server with nothing registered has an empty
      catalogue, so you will want at least one of the four library applications connected before
      anything appears on screen.
    </p>
  </Section>

  <Section title="Transcoding at library scale">
    <div class="prose">
      <h3>{TDARR.name}</h3>
      <p>{TDARR.reads}</p>
      <p>
        Keeping the two apart matters. Someone pressing play should never wait behind a queue of
        library maintenance, and library maintenance should never be starved by playback. They are
        separate systems with separate priorities, and background work steps back while people are
        watching.
      </p>
    </div>
  </Section>

  <Section tint title="Metadata and artwork">
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        Artwork and metadata identifiers arrive with the records Streamarr reads from your library
        managers. Artwork is fetched once and cached on your own disk, so browsing does not hammer
        an upstream service every time a poster scrolls past.
      </p>
      <p>
        This site and any Streamarr installation showing that imagery carry the required notice —
        see <a href="/legal/licences">licences and attribution</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </Section>

  <Section title="Setting them up">
    <p class="lede">
      Each connection is a URL and an API key, added from the admin console. The setup guide walks
      through each one and explains what to expect on the first synchronisation.
    </p>
    <p><ButtonLink href="/docs/library-managers">Connect your library managers</ButtonLink></p>
  </Section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .statement {
    max-width: var(--width-prose);
    padding: var(--space-6);
    border: 1px solid var(--line);
    border-radius: var(--radius-lg);
    background: var(--surface);
  }

  .statement p:last-child {
    margin-bottom: 0;
  }

  tbody th {
    font-size: var(--text-sm);
    font-weight: 700;
    letter-spacing: normal;
    text-transform: none;
    color: var(--ink);
  }

  .muted {
    color: var(--ink-muted);
  }

  .prose {
    max-width: var(--width-prose);
  }

  .prose h3 {
    margin-bottom: var(--space-3);
  }

  .after {
    margin-top: var(--space-6);
    margin-bottom: 0;
    max-width: var(--measure);
    color: var(--ink-soft);
    font-size: var(--text-sm);
  }
</style>
